WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:01.359
[ANNOUNCEMENT]

00:00:01.359 --> 00:00:02.359
[ANNOUNCEMENT]

00:00:02.359 --> 00:00:15.299
JENDAYI FRAZER: We're going to start a little
early.

00:00:15.299 --> 00:00:19.010
I think we have a critical mass here.

00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:23.710
There's more tents out there, but we really
do have a full house and we want to make the

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:26.870
most of this opportunity.

00:00:26.870 --> 00:00:37.570
So let me start with a, you know, customary
welcome to today's Council on Foreign Relations

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:43.710
meeting, but also the reminder that we would
ask if you could please completely turn off

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:50.260
and not just put on vibrate your cell phones,
your BlackBerrys and all wireless devices,

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:53.660
to avoid interference with the sound system.

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:56.190
As a reminder, this meeting is on the record.

00:00:56.190 --> 00:01:00.760
It is being taped, and it will be put up on
the website.

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:07.960
It is with great honor and pleasure that I
introduce our speaker to the Africa Roundtable,

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:18.159
Ms. Obi Ezekwesili, or Obiageli Ezekwesili,
who is a VP for the World Bank's Africa Region

00:01:18.159 --> 00:01:29.660
since 2007, in which she oversees 47 countries,
1,600 staff, and in 2010 was responsible for

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:40.640
$11.5 billion in new lending projects in Sub-Saharan
Africa and 1.15 billion U.S. dollars in grants.

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:45.850
Prior to coming to the World Bank, Obi was
the minister of education of the government

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:49.210
of Nigeria from 2006 to 2007.

00:01:49.210 --> 00:01:55.950
And prior to that, she was minister of solid
minerals development from 2005, 2006.

00:01:55.950 --> 00:02:01.740
She has also been in the private sector at
Deloitte &amp; Touche as an auditor, management

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:03.710
and financial services consultant.

00:02:03.710 --> 00:02:09.289
She was a founding director of Transparency
International and the director for their Africa

00:02:09.289 --> 00:02:16.090
branch from 1994 to 1999, and chairperson
of the Nigerian Extractive Industries Transparency

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:17.840
Initiative in 2004.

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:22.730
There are many more positions that Obi has
held.

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:26.080
And you can see some of those in the written
bio.

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:29.200
She is a chartered accountant.

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:33.150
She received her master's in international
law and diplomacy from the University of Lagos,

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:38.060
her master's in public policy and administration
from Harvard University at the Kennedy School

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:45.319
of Government where Obi and I met, as well
as her bachelor's degree from the University

00:02:45.319 --> 00:02:46.319
of Nigeria.

00:02:46.319 --> 00:02:51.160
She will be speaking, having worn many different
hats, so I know that we're going to have an

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:57.270
outstanding dialogue today, working in the
private sector, in the public sector for international

00:02:57.270 --> 00:03:00.099
financial institutions and for international
civil society.

00:03:00.099 --> 00:03:05.280
And so, Obi, we really look forward to your
remarks and the dialogue following.

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:07.459
Over to you.

00:03:07.459 --> 00:03:10.360
&gt;&gt;OBIGELI EZEKWESILI: Thank you.

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:11.730
Is this on?

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:12.730
Yes.

00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:15.590
Well, thank you very much, Jendayi.

00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:19.280
This is a long way back from 2000.

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:27.930
My famous story with Jendayi was how in 2000
we, having done a course of study called "Crafting

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:34.560
U.S.-African Foreign Policy," hardly did we
realize that that work together was going

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:37.239
to be implemented by you, so to speak.

00:03:37.239 --> 00:03:45.220
Because you know, a lot of what we were talking
about concerning Africa, U.S. foreign policy,

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:50.909
ultimately became what you had to use in shaping
your period in the Bush administration.

00:03:50.909 --> 00:03:56.849
And I do want to acknowledge the great work
that you did during your time as assistant

00:03:56.849 --> 00:04:00.440
secretary for Africa, and for the partnership
that we had.

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:07.420
And it was strong enough and did result in
some tangible good across the continent.

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:14.090
I want to thank you all for the opportunity
of addressing you this afternoon.

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:21.069
And I do want to say that we are in a world
that using the word "precarious" is almost

00:04:21.069 --> 00:04:28.259
an understatement considering that we frankly
do not know what this evening would bring

00:04:28.259 --> 00:04:33.000
in terms of the uncertainties that we all
have to manage.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:39.939
Whether they be countries or they be (friends
?) or they be communities or even the individual

00:04:39.939 --> 00:04:47.860
citizen, managing uncertainty and risk has
become an important subject of life.

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:58.539
That being the case, when recently the bank
did our economic update and came with the

00:04:58.539 --> 00:05:07.909
sense that it is less (cheery ?) than anyone
would hope since the last time that our focus

00:05:07.909 --> 00:05:09.150
came out.

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:18.889
It pointed to the fact that we are in waters
that will remain very difficult to manage

00:05:18.889 --> 00:05:20.639
for a long time.

00:05:20.639 --> 00:05:29.800
However, the prospect of optimism in the light
of all of these is what drives me to the importance

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:38.219
of the conversation around the place of Africa
as a continent within these global uncertainties

00:05:38.219 --> 00:05:40.240
that we must manage.

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:48.139
Let us focus for the global economy got revised
downward, and we basically say that we're

00:05:48.139 --> 00:05:57.979
going to face slower pace of expansion, 2.5
percent in rate of growth globally.

00:05:57.979 --> 00:06:02.039
Europe appears to have entered a recession.

00:06:02.039 --> 00:06:13.819
And the euro forecast is seeing to -- leads
to a contraction to about 0.3 percent for

00:06:13.819 --> 00:06:16.110
the year 2012.

00:06:16.110 --> 00:06:22.379
In the same vein, the forecast for developing
countries, which used to really be the good

00:06:22.379 --> 00:06:29.240
news in most of the forecast in the past,
is that it has slowed, and it's also facing

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:36.199
its own serious challenges of managing the
global contraction.

00:06:36.199 --> 00:06:45.389
The forecast is that it will be 5.4 percent
and 6 percent in 2012, 2013, respectively.

00:06:45.389 --> 00:06:51.639
This is of course down from the 6 percent
and 6.3 percent barely less than a year ago

00:06:51.639 --> 00:06:53.409
in our forecasting.

00:06:53.409 --> 00:07:03.550
The 2012 annual meeting of the World Economic
Forum picks up on these very precarious contexts

00:07:03.550 --> 00:07:07.889
within which global growth is being observed.

00:07:07.889 --> 00:07:11.270
And the topic says "the great transformation."

00:07:11.270 --> 00:07:18.679
It's amazing how people come up with topics
of this kind at the times of very difficult

00:07:18.679 --> 00:07:21.149
scenarios.

00:07:21.149 --> 00:07:27.499
And it says "shaping new models" -- the great
transformation, shaping new models.

00:07:27.499 --> 00:07:36.459
And part of what I read out of the statement
of -- (inaudible) -- was basically calling

00:07:36.459 --> 00:07:43.369
to question some of the models, some of the
things that we've been so used to, some of

00:07:43.369 --> 00:07:49.159
the structures and some of the concepts with
which we have worked, and questioning whether

00:07:49.159 --> 00:07:55.809
the paradigms that we have known continue
to be relevant to the imagined scenarios that

00:07:55.809 --> 00:07:57.960
we're all faced with.

00:07:57.960 --> 00:08:05.580
And it basically is saying that there was
need for leadership at different levels around

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:10.610
the world to begin to redefine what the future
could be.

00:08:10.610 --> 00:08:13.800
As I read it, it reminded me of something.

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:18.759
I was home recently -- and you know, I come
from Nigeria, and so we've been facing quite

00:08:18.759 --> 00:08:21.209
some challenges in Nigeria.

00:08:21.209 --> 00:08:27.699
And one of the young people on television
said something about some of the elders that

00:08:27.699 --> 00:08:29.490
were part of the conversation.

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:35.250
He said, why do you want to be the voice that
gives the solution to a future that you will

00:08:35.250 --> 00:08:36.250
not be part of?

00:08:36.250 --> 00:08:39.949
And I thought this is is so morbid!

00:08:39.949 --> 00:08:40.949
(Laughter.)

00:08:40.949 --> 00:08:48.029
But when that young child said that, it got
me thinking that, truly, we must be careful

00:08:48.029 --> 00:08:55.410
how much we assume that we still have the
solutions to what the future will be.

00:08:55.410 --> 00:09:03.029
I think that part of the concept of moving
toward the new global reality is going to

00:09:03.029 --> 00:09:10.459
be how much of the voice of those to whom
that future belongs to we make as a part of

00:09:10.459 --> 00:09:12.300
the conversation.

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:17.050
So as I look around the table, I'm thinking
to myself, we do need to have a Council on

00:09:17.050 --> 00:09:24.529
Foreign Relations that will begin to look
at those in their 20s as important contributors

00:09:24.529 --> 00:09:30.890
to the imagined world and imagined reality
that we're going to be facing.

00:09:30.890 --> 00:09:38.379
The Global Issues Group, which comprises leaders
of the world's multilateral and regional institutions,

00:09:38.379 --> 00:09:44.220
put its foot forward by affirming that the
solutions to realizing the objectives for

00:09:44.220 --> 00:09:50.620
strengthening growth, employment and quality
of life in every part of the world, and indeed

00:09:50.620 --> 00:09:58.589
for resolving severe challenges faced by the
global economy, would lie in economic transformation

00:09:58.589 --> 00:10:06.730
that will happen in developing countries -- in
developing countries, meaning that whatever,

00:10:06.730 --> 00:10:11.980
however you define "developing countries"
of which Africa occupies a very important

00:10:11.980 --> 00:10:19.670
space, working out of the reality that we
confront today is going to very much depend

00:10:19.670 --> 00:10:27.100
on how the solution is centered within the
problems that they face.

00:10:27.100 --> 00:10:37.681
And so for us, the global economic trend seems
to suggest that the developing world is no

00:10:37.681 --> 00:10:43.189
longer the one with the monopoly to risk.

00:10:43.189 --> 00:10:50.730
So clearly, the address where risk resides
has gone global.

00:10:50.730 --> 00:10:57.060
And to the extent that investment risk, the
new risk reality, has emerged as one that

00:10:57.060 --> 00:11:03.379
is ubiquitous, one that is less associated
with just the developing world of particularly

00:11:03.379 --> 00:11:12.620
a continent like Africa, the macro trends
that we see and the landscape that we observe

00:11:12.620 --> 00:11:18.910
means that for boardrooms across the globe
that are seeking the unbeaten path for capital

00:11:18.910 --> 00:11:28.600
flows, it's going to be important how Africa
and the rest of the developing world is considered.

00:11:28.600 --> 00:11:34.170
Your topic said Africa is the next frontier.

00:11:34.170 --> 00:11:42.420
I believe that -- in Africa, recently I was
taken to task by a very vigorous young one.

00:11:42.420 --> 00:11:45.600
You know, this is the era of the young, frankly.

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:49.589
You better prepare yourself anytime you're
speaking to them.

00:11:49.589 --> 00:11:55.930
They said, you know, the idea of Sub-Saharan
Africa doesn't appeal to her and we should

00:11:55.930 --> 00:12:02.259
stop calling -- saying "Sub-Saharan Africa,"
we should say "Africa South of the Sahara."

00:12:02.259 --> 00:12:04.680
And so I stand corrected by my young friend.

00:12:04.680 --> 00:12:09.600
I'm sure she's probably listening, so she
knows I took the correction.

00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:17.070
It is not a surprise that more and more people
are beginning to see the opportunities on

00:12:17.070 --> 00:12:19.009
the continent.

00:12:19.009 --> 00:12:25.360
The fact is that as an investment frontier,
Africa's story remains a very complex one.

00:12:25.360 --> 00:12:35.660
It is not a "single story," using the word
of my fellow compatriot Chimamanda Adichie,

00:12:35.660 --> 00:12:40.680
who cautions against the single-story complex.

00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:50.740
Some of the continent has shown clearly that
it is not one to be associated with the stories

00:12:50.740 --> 00:12:58.990
on civil wars, military takeover and insurgencies
and genocides and all of the things that defined

00:12:58.990 --> 00:13:07.970
the old Africa that some continue to insist
is the only picture of the continent that

00:13:07.970 --> 00:13:08.970
they see.

00:13:08.970 --> 00:13:17.190
The reality is that there's an aspect of Africa
that has emerged, and within the last decade,

00:13:17.190 --> 00:13:23.800
clearly, one that has made giant strides toward
more-open society.

00:13:23.800 --> 00:13:32.420
And so the concept of openness and the concept
of democratic traditions have overridden some

00:13:32.420 --> 00:13:41.990
of the well-worn -- (inaudible) -- of a continent
that was considered a basket case for its

00:13:41.990 --> 00:13:51.660
inability to build the right systems and structures
and institutions that underpinned free elections

00:13:51.660 --> 00:13:59.470
and successful economic strategies, and therefore
provided growth and (provide a reduction ?) for

00:13:59.470 --> 00:14:01.300
citizens.

00:14:01.300 --> 00:14:07.470
Beyond even the progress on the democratic
space with many more elections and transitions

00:14:07.470 --> 00:14:15.309
that have happened successfully is also the
fact that the openness has resulted in a better

00:14:15.309 --> 00:14:28.499
embrace of the economic imperatives of macroeconomic
stability and the basic trust that the fundamentals

00:14:28.499 --> 00:14:34.800
are not any different as far as economic growth
is concerned.

00:14:34.800 --> 00:14:45.800
And that if Africa understood and embraced
the tradition of working through market principles

00:14:45.800 --> 00:14:53.319
and defining the roles of government and the
roles of the private sector appropriately,

00:14:53.319 --> 00:15:01.379
that Africa, paying attention to the key fundamental
issues of economic policy, could grow.

00:15:01.379 --> 00:15:09.559
And so we saw in the last decade a continent
that made peace with the embrace of the market

00:15:09.559 --> 00:15:12.660
as an important factor for growth.

00:15:12.660 --> 00:15:19.529
An eminent business person did say that profit
lies where the gap between perception and

00:15:19.529 --> 00:15:21.529
reality is greatest.

00:15:21.529 --> 00:15:23.759
He's not alone in that.

00:15:23.759 --> 00:15:25.610
I mean, think of it.

00:15:25.610 --> 00:15:36.829
The Economist, a magazine that I grew up on,
because I had a dad who was one of their greatest

00:15:36.829 --> 00:15:43.319
fans, but I had to go -- I had to dump The
Economist for a while because it woke up one

00:15:43.319 --> 00:15:51.579
day and decided that an entire continent could
be called "hopeless continent."

00:15:51.579 --> 00:15:56.149
And it did this in 2000, it had that caption
and said "hopeless continent."

00:15:56.149 --> 00:15:57.149
Why?

00:15:57.149 --> 00:16:04.699
It was mirroring basically that perception
that it was a continent that many assumed

00:16:04.699 --> 00:16:09.440
would never find its feet in the global scheme
of things.

00:16:09.440 --> 00:16:19.519
Well, you know, there's nothing like a -- (inaudible)
-- to give you a different set of wisdom.

00:16:19.519 --> 00:16:28.550
And so within the last decade, as Africa began
to show that it is indeed capable of taking

00:16:28.550 --> 00:16:35.950
hold of the key economic concepts and translating
the poor performance that it was associated

00:16:35.950 --> 00:16:45.480
with into better performance on the economic
space, we now see that the 2011 edition of

00:16:45.480 --> 00:16:52.959
the same Economist points to it and says we
recant of what we said of Africa a decade

00:16:52.959 --> 00:16:53.959
ago.

00:16:53.959 --> 00:16:58.680
We do recant of it; and therefore, we now
say Africa rising.

00:16:58.680 --> 00:17:06.409
Now, Africa rising is a good story, is a good
caption, but it's important to our piecing

00:17:06.409 --> 00:17:14.720
together where Africa is rising from and where
it is supposed to be rising to.

00:17:14.720 --> 00:17:26.529
In Africa today, we see the brightest possibilities
as being in the fact that you have the continent

00:17:26.529 --> 00:17:32.380
driving its economic vision.

00:17:32.380 --> 00:17:41.580
The economic vision of the continent has become
one that the continent itself is defining,

00:17:41.580 --> 00:17:50.770
which is quite so different from what happened
in the past where there was almost a confusion

00:17:50.770 --> 00:17:56.860
as to what the vision of economic development
was supposed to be.

00:17:56.860 --> 00:18:03.840
In the interaction that one has with the leadership
and the citizens of the continent, that matter

00:18:03.840 --> 00:18:12.210
of taking ownership of an economic vision
is so key, because then it determines that

00:18:12.210 --> 00:18:21.250
the level of commitment and investment into
realizing that vision that both the leadership

00:18:21.250 --> 00:18:28.570
as well as the citizens are willing to offer.

00:18:28.570 --> 00:18:37.121
In the last decade, what we saw was basically
an Africa that made peace with the concept

00:18:37.121 --> 00:18:41.480
of macroeconomic stability as being fundamental
for growth.

00:18:41.480 --> 00:18:48.960
Now, once that happened, and that's about
taking ownership of the economic vision, you

00:18:48.960 --> 00:18:57.010
did not need to tell any leader that the kind
of fiscal recklessness and rascality that

00:18:57.010 --> 00:19:04.270
was associated with economic behavior in the
past would undermine the economic vision of

00:19:04.270 --> 00:19:06.230
growth and poverty reduction.

00:19:06.230 --> 00:19:08.970
They lanced that.

00:19:08.970 --> 00:19:16.500
You began to see management of the economy,
of the macroeconomy, both on the fiscal and

00:19:16.500 --> 00:19:27.250
on the macro side from the perspective of
guarding that economic reality of the necessity

00:19:27.250 --> 00:19:28.660
for macroeconomic stability.

00:19:28.660 --> 00:19:30.810
Now, why is that important?

00:19:30.810 --> 00:19:40.000
It's important because when the crisis of
2008 commenced around the world, the continent

00:19:40.000 --> 00:19:48.260
that kept with macroeconomic stability the
most was Africa.

00:19:48.260 --> 00:19:57.919
Africa kept with it while other parts of the
world began to have loser economic policy.

00:19:57.919 --> 00:20:06.380
Many of the leaders on the continent held
on tightly to fiscal prudence and to ensuring

00:20:06.380 --> 00:20:13.760
that the management of the macroeconomy -- inflation,
interest rate, foreign exchange -- the management

00:20:13.760 --> 00:20:18.370
of it was guided seriously by this continent.

00:20:18.370 --> 00:20:27.149
And what we see since 2008, even at a time
when they have less fiscal space today than

00:20:27.149 --> 00:20:36.559
they had at the outset of the crisis in 2008,
is a continuing to guard that space.

00:20:36.559 --> 00:20:49.200
So I make a big thing out of Africa accepting
an economic truth and owning it as being important.

00:20:49.200 --> 00:20:56.220
The reason I can say that very, very vigorously
is that I work within an institution which

00:20:56.220 --> 00:21:04.399
for many years, many decades was looked on
by most of Africa with suspicion as being

00:21:04.399 --> 00:21:09.580
institutions that wants to impose ideas on
Africa.

00:21:09.580 --> 00:21:16.020
Well, the World Bank doesn't need to say that
macroeconomic stability is important, to any

00:21:16.020 --> 00:21:18.870
leader anymore on the continent.

00:21:18.870 --> 00:21:24.060
They finally got that, and they are doing
it themselves.

00:21:24.060 --> 00:21:30.510
Some of them are even doing -- holding on
tightly to it more than the expectation that

00:21:30.510 --> 00:21:33.419
Bretton Woods institutions want of them.

00:21:33.419 --> 00:21:41.080
That's key, because to be able to achieve
the macro stability, the next level becomes

00:21:41.080 --> 00:21:49.080
your second-generation reforms, and that's
where the difficulty shows up.

00:21:49.080 --> 00:21:56.080
Because what it took -- the great investment
it took to get to the point where African

00:21:56.080 --> 00:22:03.570
leaders and policy practitioners understood
the necessity and the fundamental need for

00:22:03.570 --> 00:22:10.970
macroeconomic stability and to hold on so
tightly to it with good effect.

00:22:10.970 --> 00:22:18.320
Because as I speak, of all the continents,
Africa's rate of growth before the crisis

00:22:18.320 --> 00:22:26.010
in 2008 had become an average of 5.7 percent,
sharply declined to 1.9 percent at the height

00:22:26.010 --> 00:22:27.010
of the crisis.

00:22:27.010 --> 00:22:35.779
But 2010, it's started a trend up back from
1.9 percent and ended at 3.80 percent.

00:22:35.779 --> 00:22:40.470
By 2011, we're talking about 4.3 percent.

00:22:40.470 --> 00:22:48.210
And by -- it's just continuing to be on the
upward growth.

00:22:48.210 --> 00:22:58.029
Our sense is that, excluding South Africa,
the regional average for Africa's growth would

00:22:58.029 --> 00:23:04.350
be 5.9 percent, although with South Africa
it ends as 4.9 percent.

00:23:04.350 --> 00:23:12.809
Now, that's significant in a world where we're
seeing such low growth levels in the most-advanced

00:23:12.809 --> 00:23:14.149
of economies.

00:23:14.149 --> 00:23:18.960
So this puts it on the path immediately next
to the Chinas and the Indias.

00:23:18.960 --> 00:23:26.669
And that's important to have as a perspective
as we look at Africa's role going forward.

00:23:26.669 --> 00:23:35.220
The great story for Africa sometimes is made
to appear as a single story by people who

00:23:35.220 --> 00:23:41.299
say, OK, yes, so maybe the growth is because
of the expansion in the commodities market;

00:23:41.299 --> 00:23:44.309
and therefore, that's what's driving the growth.

00:23:44.309 --> 00:23:51.450
But I want to clearly state that as we decompose
the sources of the growth, what we find is

00:23:51.450 --> 00:23:56.929
that only one -- (inaudible) -- can be accounted
for by the commodities story.

00:23:56.929 --> 00:24:06.130
The rest of it is actually about macroeconomic
reforms and the investment in the second-generation

00:24:06.130 --> 00:24:13.750
reforms that are supposed to spur growth through
better economic policymaking.

00:24:13.750 --> 00:24:17.970
This growth story is not enough.

00:24:17.970 --> 00:24:25.429
The growth story is not enough because the
growth story is one that excludes quite a

00:24:25.429 --> 00:24:31.210
huge population on the continent, that must
benefit from growth.

00:24:31.210 --> 00:24:37.730
And that's where the role of the World Bank
and other institutions and partners of Africa

00:24:37.730 --> 00:24:47.370
in focusing on how the growth can become stronger
and the growth can become broader, and therefore

00:24:47.370 --> 00:24:54.760
growth that can be shared by quite a larger
population of citizens, where 50 percent are

00:24:54.760 --> 00:25:00.200
still in the category of the poor, becomes
the next-most-important agenda.

00:25:00.200 --> 00:25:07.519
And that's where the issue I alluded to on
structural reforms, the second-generation

00:25:07.519 --> 00:25:10.090
forms, becomes key.

00:25:10.090 --> 00:25:16.230
And that's where, if you say Africa is the
next frontier for growth, where would the

00:25:16.230 --> 00:25:18.240
growth be coming from?

00:25:18.240 --> 00:25:25.659
One important source of that growth, besides
the good economic policy, is going to be how

00:25:25.659 --> 00:25:33.820
you focus on the right kinds of investments
in sectors where the good policymaking is

00:25:33.820 --> 00:25:34.820
happening.

00:25:34.820 --> 00:25:43.260
And my proposition is that agriculture and
agribusiness is an important area of focus

00:25:43.260 --> 00:25:47.429
for Africa's economic growth.

00:25:47.429 --> 00:25:55.789
Agriculture and agribusiness, the value-added
prospects that they hold and the fact that

00:25:55.789 --> 00:26:02.850
it is still a continent where more than 70
percent are in this sector.

00:26:02.850 --> 00:26:12.450
Whatever Africa does with other sectors of
opportunities, agriculture has to be a key

00:26:12.450 --> 00:26:20.360
component of Africa's readiness to embrace
private capital of the magnitude and beyond

00:26:20.360 --> 00:26:27.590
what we have seen in its embrace of policies
and the right public investment that opened

00:26:27.590 --> 00:26:31.450
up the information and communication technologies
sector.

00:26:31.450 --> 00:26:39.490
The telecom sector has been an incredible
success on the continent, but the opportunity

00:26:39.490 --> 00:26:47.330
to benefit from growth for the average citizen
on the continent is going to be through what

00:26:47.330 --> 00:26:49.940
we do with agriculture.

00:26:49.940 --> 00:26:57.450
And so in looking at agriculture, we say that
Africa has one-quarter of he world's -- (inaudible)

00:26:57.450 --> 00:26:58.450
-- land.

00:26:58.450 --> 00:27:04.659
In fact, 50 percent of the outstanding is
in Africa.

00:27:04.659 --> 00:27:12.480
Now, if Africa had the right policies and
the right investments into this sector, Africa

00:27:12.480 --> 00:27:17.880
would become part of the solution to global
food demand.

00:27:17.880 --> 00:27:27.210
And we believe that the demand for food, which
will reach about $100 billion by, you know,

00:27:27.210 --> 00:27:34.529
doubling the level by 2015, doubling the level
that it was in 2000, creates a huge opportunity

00:27:34.529 --> 00:27:41.269
for an African green revolution that looks
at appropriate investment in both the agricultural

00:27:41.269 --> 00:27:48.380
skills as well as the infrastructure that
would necessitate the kind of growth that

00:27:48.380 --> 00:27:55.990
is held up in the dormant sector of agriculture,
or the under-productive.

00:27:55.990 --> 00:28:03.600
The productivity of the sector is so critical
for Africa to truly emerge as that next frontier

00:28:03.600 --> 00:28:07.519
that the topic seeks to explore.

00:28:07.519 --> 00:28:12.549
Sub-Saharan Africa does already have many
success stories in agriculture.

00:28:12.549 --> 00:28:20.399
The production of, say, cassava chips in places
like Ghana, or organic coffee in a place like

00:28:20.399 --> 00:28:27.799
Tanzania, or cut flowers in Kenya, as well
as agriculture in Malawi, all of these products

00:28:27.799 --> 00:28:34.060
are today being exported to European and other
Western countries.

00:28:34.060 --> 00:28:44.669
Expanding markets, such as their reproduction
in East Africa, is going to be a formidable

00:28:44.669 --> 00:28:50.160
opportunity for Africa as a frontier market.

00:28:50.160 --> 00:28:59.500
And the good thing about agriculture is the
fact that it really gets to the heart of poverty

00:28:59.500 --> 00:29:01.340
on the continent.

00:29:01.340 --> 00:29:10.440
For every one farmer in the coco sector of,
say, Cote d'Ivoire, they are responsible the

00:29:10.440 --> 00:29:18.480
health and education of a multiple of 10 people
in that country.

00:29:18.480 --> 00:29:25.071
So imagine the impact, the quick impact on
poverty, because access to health, access

00:29:25.071 --> 00:29:33.190
to education through improved income of the
farmer means a direct intervention, not waiting

00:29:33.190 --> 00:29:35.430
for development aid.

00:29:35.430 --> 00:29:42.210
But it's actually about families making the
right kinds of decisions about investing in

00:29:42.210 --> 00:29:50.120
their children, and therefore improving Africa's
human capital, which essentially is the most

00:29:50.120 --> 00:29:58.450
important article of fate that we must all
subscribe to concerning the continent.

00:29:58.450 --> 00:30:07.669
That it is not Africa's extractive industries,
but Africa's population, properly educated,

00:30:07.669 --> 00:30:17.970
given the right tools to solve economic problems
themselves, that will take the continent higher

00:30:17.970 --> 00:30:24.870
than where it currently stays in the ranking
of nations as far as economic performance

00:30:24.870 --> 00:30:26.750
is concerned.

00:30:26.750 --> 00:30:32.259
One of the things that we've been watching
closely is the interrelatedness of social

00:30:32.259 --> 00:30:40.140
and demographic changes and how all of these
create the domestic engines for growth.

00:30:40.140 --> 00:30:45.140
Key among this is, of course, urbanization
and the expanding labor force and the rise

00:30:45.140 --> 00:30:48.440
of the middle-class African consumer.

00:30:48.440 --> 00:30:53.920
In 1980, just 28 percent of Africans lived
in cities.

00:30:53.920 --> 00:31:00.420
Today, 40 percent of the continent's 1 billion
people live in cities, and this is comparable

00:31:00.420 --> 00:31:05.950
to what you have in China and in India, in
fact larger than what you have in India.

00:31:05.950 --> 00:31:10.759
By 2030, that share is expected to rise to
50 percent.

00:31:10.759 --> 00:31:18.779
And Africa's top 18 cities will have a combined
spending power of $1.3 trillion.

00:31:18.779 --> 00:31:24.019
At the same time, Africa's labor force is
expanding, in contrast to what is happening

00:31:24.019 --> 00:31:25.710
in the rest of the world.

00:31:25.710 --> 00:31:30.870
So while the rest of the world is facing aging
populations, Africa is becoming much more

00:31:30.870 --> 00:31:33.080
youthful, much more vibrant.

00:31:33.080 --> 00:31:37.419
The continent has more than 500 million people
of working age.

00:31:37.419 --> 00:31:44.720
By 2040, the number is projected to exceed
1.1 billion, more than in China or India.

00:31:44.720 --> 00:31:51.149
And these people are going to be the ones
that lift the GDP of the continent.

00:31:51.149 --> 00:31:57.169
But lifting that GDP means that the investment
in the young has to start today.

00:31:57.169 --> 00:32:03.060
It's a double-edged sword to have a youthful
population.

00:32:03.060 --> 00:32:10.169
It's either we're investing in this youthful
population, converting them to the human capital

00:32:10.169 --> 00:32:15.120
that would determine the innovations and the
solutions of the present and the future, or

00:32:15.120 --> 00:32:26.659
we would have a situation where they would
become the underbelly of economies that never

00:32:26.659 --> 00:32:30.330
go beyond their current performance.

00:32:30.330 --> 00:32:35.700
And in fact, they would become sources of
destabilization on the continent.

00:32:35.700 --> 00:32:41.679
Disruption would necessarily happen where
the citizens, especially these youthful ones,

00:32:41.679 --> 00:32:51.350
do not have the opportunity to express themselves
as economic agents, which is what is encouraged

00:32:51.350 --> 00:32:58.299
through the greater openness of the continent
to open economic principles.

00:32:58.299 --> 00:33:07.029
Of course, an important theme that we need
to get in mind is that climate change is a

00:33:07.029 --> 00:33:10.360
risk that faces Africa.

00:33:10.360 --> 00:33:24.370
Even though Africa's contribution to global
warming isn't as significant as you may, which

00:33:24.370 --> 00:33:34.750
is 0.2 percent -- .02 percent, the fact is
that the impact of the climate change, climate

00:33:34.750 --> 00:33:38.750
variation, weather variation is already huge
on the continent.

00:33:38.750 --> 00:33:45.580
Whether it is in what you have followed in
the Sahara region of Africa, or it's in the

00:33:45.580 --> 00:33:52.070
coastal erosions or in the (decertification
?) that's happening with such frequency, and

00:33:52.070 --> 00:33:58.320
the heat waves and the storms and the floods,
all of our different parts of the continent,

00:33:58.320 --> 00:34:05.919
it is clear that agriculture stands a risk,
even though we have a valuable proposition

00:34:05.919 --> 00:34:09.290
that leads to that real growth.

00:34:09.290 --> 00:34:18.090
Because when you look at the comparative-advantage-embracing
approach for economic policy, and agriculture

00:34:18.090 --> 00:34:24.070
is it, then anything that threatens agriculture
fundamentally like climate change becomes

00:34:24.070 --> 00:34:29.360
an important element for focus by the African
policymaker.

00:34:29.360 --> 00:34:37.700
And so beginning to look at this as something
that practitioners on the continent will pay

00:34:37.700 --> 00:34:40.680
attention to is critical.

00:34:40.680 --> 00:34:49.500
I do want to say that there are many issues
that we can learn from.

00:34:49.500 --> 00:34:57.630
In the improvements that we've seen in investment
flows into Africa, taking the telecom sector

00:34:57.630 --> 00:35:05.490
as an example, what we have learned is that
the right public policy preceded the flow

00:35:05.490 --> 00:35:06.790
of investment.

00:35:06.790 --> 00:35:18.260
Now, with that lesson, it means that outstanding
areas of reform in energy, in transportation,

00:35:18.260 --> 00:35:29.410
in agriculture, in health, in basic environment
and sustainable development and all that that

00:35:29.410 --> 00:35:36.870
is associated with it, we need a strong focus
on the right sets of policies -- the right

00:35:36.870 --> 00:35:43.200
sets of policies, the right sets of legal
and regulatory systems, the right sets of

00:35:43.200 --> 00:35:47.130
definition of rules of government as well
as the private sector.

00:35:47.130 --> 00:35:53.460
But even more important is an understanding
of how innovation happens.

00:35:53.460 --> 00:36:02.170
When the telecom sector and the ICT took off
on the continent, hardly did anyone realize

00:36:02.170 --> 00:36:10.310
how a country like Kenya, with its youthful
population, would be the first set of people

00:36:10.310 --> 00:36:19.240
that would take this access to information
as an important tool for economic interaction.

00:36:19.240 --> 00:36:29.810
And so the (impasse ?), the mobile money today
is associated with the innovation in Kenya.

00:36:29.810 --> 00:36:35.640
The good thing is it's an example of how innovation
can be driven within the continent.

00:36:35.640 --> 00:36:42.890
Because at the World Bank, we actually support
through the (South-South ?) lending the provision

00:36:42.890 --> 00:36:48.700
of the lessons of the (impasse ?) to countries
in Asia.

00:36:48.700 --> 00:36:52.810
So Africa is a home for innovation.

00:36:52.810 --> 00:36:58.410
What it means is that, as we look at energy
and we look at agriculture, what are the innovations

00:36:58.410 --> 00:37:01.010
that could come out of all of this?

00:37:01.010 --> 00:37:08.890
But only to the extent that we can see that
interrelatedness between the policy, the necessary

00:37:08.890 --> 00:37:14.120
investment on the side of government and the
definition of a clear road for it, as well

00:37:14.120 --> 00:37:19.001
as the participation of the citizens in finding
the solutions that are appropriate for them.

00:37:19.001 --> 00:37:23.490
The challenges that remain are huge, as I
said.

00:37:23.490 --> 00:37:29.551
Less than 25 percent of Africa's population
has access to electricity, compared to 40

00:37:29.551 --> 00:37:31.840
percent in other low-income regions.

00:37:31.840 --> 00:37:38.640
The consequence is that Africa is less competitive
beyond the factory floor and remains constrained

00:37:38.640 --> 00:37:44.930
and unable to take advantage of the 2 percent
increase in growth as well as 40 percent increase

00:37:44.930 --> 00:37:49.180
in productivity levels that result from better
infrastructure.

00:37:49.180 --> 00:37:59.880
In fact, you know, when you look at the challenge
of energy on the continent, it's such an incredible

00:37:59.880 --> 00:38:02.210
obstacle to growth.

00:38:02.210 --> 00:38:11.590
And yet, we know that by reforming and structurally
changing the sector and having the right policy

00:38:11.590 --> 00:38:20.150
framework and legal systems, that the same
way that the market was drawn to the opportunity

00:38:20.150 --> 00:38:25.810
in the telecom sector, so also will the market
be drawn to the opportunity in generation,

00:38:25.810 --> 00:38:27.610
in distribution.

00:38:27.610 --> 00:38:33.880
And so what are the incentives that would
drive the process of making this sector a

00:38:33.880 --> 00:38:40.780
sector that opens up and receives the market
as they come calling?

00:38:40.780 --> 00:38:43.760
It's a huge place of opportunity.

00:38:43.760 --> 00:38:51.370
Even in the financial sector, still just less
than 30 percent of Africa has access to financial

00:38:51.370 --> 00:38:53.060
services.

00:38:53.060 --> 00:38:58.030
What does that say for a frontier market?

00:38:58.030 --> 00:39:04.030
Access to finance is the life wire of any
economy.

00:39:04.030 --> 00:39:11.840
And if there are still serious exclusion of
a huge population, financial exclusion, then

00:39:11.840 --> 00:39:18.840
there is an opportunity in that space for
really bringing more people into the economic

00:39:18.840 --> 00:39:20.780
activities.

00:39:20.780 --> 00:39:25.320
It was Winston Churchill that said that the
pessimist sees difficulty in every opportunity,

00:39:25.320 --> 00:39:29.140
while the optimist sees opportunity in every
difficulty.

00:39:29.140 --> 00:39:39.240
And so in the Africa rising, Vijay Mahajan
adopted the approach and sees Africa as a

00:39:39.240 --> 00:39:48.380
home to more than 900 million consumers richer
than most people think and presenting an opportunity

00:39:48.380 --> 00:39:51.160
as big as China and India.

00:39:51.160 --> 00:39:58.750
Now, if that is taking a perspective, it shows
that the Council on Foreign Relations is having

00:39:58.750 --> 00:40:06.600
the right kind of conversation, because if
you fragmented Africa, you would not see this

00:40:06.600 --> 00:40:13.730
big essence of the market and the opportunity
it presents, which is what takes me to the

00:40:13.730 --> 00:40:21.820
issue of regional integration, because the
regional integration of the continent is really

00:40:21.820 --> 00:40:25.590
one idea whose time is well past.

00:40:25.590 --> 00:40:32.140
(What can we talk ?) of regional integration
has been more difficult than expressing the

00:40:32.140 --> 00:40:34.470
desirability of it.

00:40:34.470 --> 00:40:43.510
And so the kind of attention to the opportunities
for integrating policies and investments and

00:40:43.510 --> 00:40:51.950
identifying solutions that can be solved from
the perspective of regional goods is really

00:40:51.950 --> 00:40:59.120
germane to any conversation on Africa as a
frontier market.

00:40:59.120 --> 00:41:05.110
Markets that are too small are of less significance
than markets that are sizable.

00:41:05.110 --> 00:41:10.920
And so in the work that we do with the continent,
whether it's in the integration through infrastructure,

00:41:10.920 --> 00:41:17.670
or it's integration through policy harmonization,
or it's integration through institutions and

00:41:17.670 --> 00:41:24.560
regulatory systems, what we're pushing toward
is as driven by the leadership of the continent

00:41:24.560 --> 00:41:30.980
that it not be so separated from the domestic
policy environment.

00:41:30.980 --> 00:41:38.410
The domestic policy environment and this regional
policy environment must find places of convergence.

00:41:38.410 --> 00:41:45.620
And the further studies into the political
economy of fostering those linkages is going

00:41:45.620 --> 00:41:50.240
to be a key and an important area of focus.

00:41:50.240 --> 00:41:57.530
Most of Africa says enough with development
aid, we want trade and we want investment.

00:41:57.530 --> 00:42:02.300
And I agree, completely agree.

00:42:02.300 --> 00:42:11.510
But there is still a huge agenda for investing
to enable the right business environment on

00:42:11.510 --> 00:42:13.310
the continent.

00:42:13.310 --> 00:42:20.910
Beyond the reform of the business environment
in the -- (inaudible) -- of investment climate

00:42:20.910 --> 00:42:27.400
reforms, if you do not solve the energy crisis
on the continent, the cost of doing business

00:42:27.400 --> 00:42:32.400
on the continent remains really prohibitive.

00:42:32.400 --> 00:42:39.450
If you do not solve the transportation issues,
if you do not invest and produce the right

00:42:39.450 --> 00:42:47.910
variety of skills on the continent, the cost
of labor continues to be -- will be prohibitive.

00:42:47.910 --> 00:42:53.950
Then the population is not an advantage, because
side-by-side a population are opportunities

00:42:53.950 --> 00:42:57.670
for work that will be taken up by others.

00:42:57.670 --> 00:43:05.380
And so all of this means that both development
assistance, trade and investment must all

00:43:05.380 --> 00:43:15.750
come together as a package that will support
Africa in emerging into that frontier that

00:43:15.750 --> 00:43:18.710
we all agree it must emerge into.

00:43:18.710 --> 00:43:28.110
The role of the Diaspora as part of that strategy
for mobilizing as much resources for Africa's

00:43:28.110 --> 00:43:31.080
investment as possible is key.

00:43:31.080 --> 00:43:37.940
There are some of the countries in Africa
today where the Diaspora flows whenever the

00:43:37.940 --> 00:43:43.130
decline become a business for balance-of-payment
crisis.

00:43:43.130 --> 00:43:45.480
It's become that important.

00:43:45.480 --> 00:43:53.540
And so how the transition that would begin
to happen as you see more of the country -- more

00:43:53.540 --> 00:44:01.740
of the development -- more of the countries
providing development assistance to Africa,

00:44:01.740 --> 00:44:08.981
as they go through the global economic crisis
and as they try to determine priorities, it's

00:44:08.981 --> 00:44:17.280
going to be important that conversations as
to how the Diaspora flows begin to solve as

00:44:17.280 --> 00:44:20.860
much more than a simple or mere social protection.

00:44:20.860 --> 00:44:30.090
Because today, the value of a lot of the Diaspora
flows is as a social safety net for the Africans

00:44:30.090 --> 00:44:34.020
that receive those money.

00:44:34.020 --> 00:44:38.970
But it has much more role for development
as we see.

00:44:38.970 --> 00:44:44.850
And so some of the conversations on Diaspora
bonds have got to be much more sophisticated

00:44:44.850 --> 00:44:49.570
than merely looking at it as an instrument
to mobilize resources.

00:44:49.570 --> 00:44:57.590
But to coherently defining the space that
those resources would take in helping to accelerate

00:44:57.590 --> 00:45:07.040
and push up growth in particularly the sectors
that are key factors for growth is an idea

00:45:07.040 --> 00:45:08.070
that's right on the table.

00:45:08.070 --> 00:45:14.730
Our estimate is that remittances are more
than double official aid received by developing

00:45:14.730 --> 00:45:21.110
countries, reaching about 325 billion (dollars)
in 2010, of which more than 20 billion (dollars)

00:45:21.110 --> 00:45:22.110
flows to Africa.

00:45:22.110 --> 00:45:27.070
Now, $20 billion is significant resources
flowing as Diaspora resources.

00:45:27.070 --> 00:45:37.310
For this reason, we are focused on work that
reduces the transfer costs of worker remittances

00:45:37.310 --> 00:45:45.230
and work that helps to look at them for their
impact on growth and poverty reduction by

00:45:45.230 --> 00:45:53.540
unlocking opportunities to channel those remittances
to productive investment rather than mere

00:45:53.540 --> 00:45:54.580
consumption.

00:45:54.580 --> 00:46:02.010
For investors, what I would always ask is,
do you yet have an Africa strategy?

00:46:02.010 --> 00:46:09.810
If Africa is the now and the future, then
no global player will do well by not having

00:46:09.810 --> 00:46:11.760
their Africa strategy.

00:46:11.760 --> 00:46:19.850
For partners or investors, working with the
continent means understanding the continent.

00:46:19.850 --> 00:46:28.180
And so the necessary investment in understanding
the opportunities as well as the challenges

00:46:28.180 --> 00:46:35.650
on the continent would go with understanding
the citizens and the voice that they express

00:46:35.650 --> 00:46:38.580
about where they see the continent going.

00:46:38.580 --> 00:46:45.360
And that takes me to the importance of the
civil society and the voice of the citizens

00:46:45.360 --> 00:46:46.360
in Africa.

00:46:46.360 --> 00:46:52.410
A lot of what's driving better performance
in some of the countries that we see is that

00:46:52.410 --> 00:46:59.210
demand for good governance and accountability
is becoming a pattern of relationship between

00:46:59.210 --> 00:47:02.250
those that govern and those that they govern.

00:47:02.250 --> 00:47:08.410
And so, how can we consolidate this demand
for good governance by the citizens of the

00:47:08.410 --> 00:47:09.410
continent?

00:47:09.410 --> 00:47:17.170
How can we as partners, whether in the development
world or in the private sector, encourage

00:47:17.170 --> 00:47:24.920
the kind of responsible partnership with the
civil society, with citizens group, with communities

00:47:24.920 --> 00:47:32.520
in the kind of way that governance, which
was always the key obstacle to Africa's growth,

00:47:32.520 --> 00:47:41.860
can finally be one that gets resolved through
the participation of the three sectors: by

00:47:41.860 --> 00:47:46.460
private sector, public sector and the civil
society.

00:47:46.460 --> 00:47:55.090
For us at the bank, our focus has been on
the governance agenda and the public sector

00:47:55.090 --> 00:47:58.690
as the foundation of everything that we do.

00:47:58.690 --> 00:48:07.850
We came to the awakening that even in solving
Africa's energy problem or solving its transportation

00:48:07.850 --> 00:48:16.160
problem, ultimately, it would be a governance
and institutions-building issue.

00:48:16.160 --> 00:48:27.930
And so working through that process means
that you cannot do the governance agenda with

00:48:27.930 --> 00:48:29.620
the citizens.

00:48:29.620 --> 00:48:31.760
It's ultimately about the citizens.

00:48:31.760 --> 00:48:37.670
And it's a message to those that lead the
continent.

00:48:37.670 --> 00:48:42.150
The citizens of the continent are asking for
better governance.

00:48:42.150 --> 00:48:45.150
It's not an external agenda.

00:48:45.150 --> 00:48:51.150
No one external to Africa can foster good
governance as much as the citizens can.

00:48:51.150 --> 00:48:58.010
And that space where the citizens are occupying
now and demanding on the business of greater

00:48:58.010 --> 00:49:05.730
transparency and access to information for
results from government means that everyone

00:49:05.730 --> 00:49:11.260
of us, including the Council on Foreign Relations,
should really be spending a lot of time trying

00:49:11.260 --> 00:49:18.550
to figure out what it is that we can do to
support the voice of the citizens, to strengthen

00:49:18.550 --> 00:49:23.650
that voice and that demand for good governance
and for results.

00:49:23.650 --> 00:49:30.980
Because it is in so doing that the economic
freedom and the right economic policies will

00:49:30.980 --> 00:49:39.040
come together and enable the performance that
readies Africa more and more as that frontier

00:49:39.040 --> 00:49:40.470
that no one can ignore.

00:49:40.470 --> 00:49:42.240
So thank you very much.

00:49:42.240 --> 00:49:43.240
(Applause.)

00:49:43.240 --> 00:49:48.240
&gt;&gt;FRAZER: Well, thank you, Obi.

00:49:48.240 --> 00:49:49.910
That was really quite fantastic.

00:49:49.910 --> 00:49:54.200
You've put a lot of serious substance before
us.

00:49:54.200 --> 00:49:59.040
I heard someone outside before we started
say that there's people around the world who

00:49:59.040 --> 00:50:02.390
are going to be sad that you're leaving the
World Bank.

00:50:02.390 --> 00:50:03.920
And I certainly share that sentiment.

00:50:03.920 --> 00:50:09.090
But I have another one in which I'm so happy
you're leaving the World Bank, because a force

00:50:09.090 --> 00:50:14.200
with the voice that you have and the ability
that you have, going back to Africa and working

00:50:14.200 --> 00:50:20.480
for all that you just stated here, makes me
very confident in Africa's future.

00:50:20.480 --> 00:50:26.170
And so I'm very pleased that, in many ways,
you're going to fly free and do your thing.

00:50:26.170 --> 00:50:30.460
And we're going to look forward to watching
you over the coming years.

00:50:30.460 --> 00:50:34.910
But with that, let me open the session for
question and answers.

00:50:34.910 --> 00:50:39.010
We want to invite the audience members to
join in a discussion.

00:50:39.010 --> 00:50:44.580
If you have a question, please orient your
name tent vertically so that I can see your

00:50:44.580 --> 00:50:45.970
name.

00:50:45.970 --> 00:50:50.320
When you are called upon, please state your
name and affiliation, and keep your questions

00:50:50.320 --> 00:50:55.530
as concise as possible to allow as many participants
as possible to speak.

00:50:55.530 --> 00:50:58.690
And I'm going to take three questions at a
time.

00:50:58.690 --> 00:51:03.000
And we have about 15 minutes' worth of time
for the Q&amp;A.

00:51:03.000 --> 00:51:07.470
So with that, I'm going to just go right around
the table.

00:51:07.470 --> 00:51:13.830
Mr. Robert Hertzberg and then Joel and then
Ambassador Davis.

00:51:13.830 --> 00:51:17.290
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: My question relates to good
governance.

00:51:17.290 --> 00:51:23.840
Your final comments, you indicated this is
fundamentally up to the citizens of Africa.

00:51:23.840 --> 00:51:30.220
But you also -- I heard you also call for
help from the Council on Foreign Relations

00:51:30.220 --> 00:51:33.740
and the people around the table.

00:51:33.740 --> 00:51:42.560
What is the role of outsiders in helping to
foster good governance in Africa?

00:51:42.560 --> 00:51:50.170
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: Joel Barkan, Center for Strategic
and International Studies.

00:51:50.170 --> 00:51:56.040
First, thanks very much for the first point
you made about Africa is not a single story.

00:51:56.040 --> 00:51:59.500
It's a very varied one.

00:51:59.500 --> 00:52:02.190
My questions are basically two.

00:52:02.190 --> 00:52:03.440
You talked about ownership.

00:52:03.440 --> 00:52:09.560
And at the bank, one of the mechanisms for
facilitating ownership has been budget support.

00:52:09.560 --> 00:52:16.140
And yet, there has been some stepping back
from budget support in a number of countries,

00:52:16.140 --> 00:52:18.110
Uganda in particular in recent years.

00:52:18.110 --> 00:52:22.660
I'd like you to comment about budget support
as a mechanism.

00:52:22.660 --> 00:52:29.700
And secondly, in respect to your point about
macroeconomic stability, there also appears

00:52:29.700 --> 00:52:37.470
to be some slippage in parts of the continent
-- again, Uganda, Ethiopia in particular where

00:52:37.470 --> 00:52:39.740
inflation is now 40 percent.

00:52:39.740 --> 00:52:41.310
&gt;&gt;FRAZER: Ambassador Davis.

00:52:41.310 --> 00:52:46.550
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: Ruth A. Davis, former ambassador
to the Republic of Benin.

00:52:46.550 --> 00:52:50.260
Thank you very much for your insightful, sweeping
remarks.

00:52:50.260 --> 00:52:58.280
I wish you would comment a little bit about
the role of women entrepreneurs in this sort

00:52:58.280 --> 00:53:02.170
of developing a new frontier, economic frontier.

00:53:02.170 --> 00:53:03.830
&gt;&gt;FRAZER: Thank you.

00:53:03.830 --> 00:53:04.950
&gt;&gt;EZEKWESILI: OK.

00:53:04.950 --> 00:53:07.690
So let me take these ones quickly.

00:53:07.690 --> 00:53:17.500
On the role of the outsiders, I think that
there's a lot in terms of partnerships that

00:53:17.500 --> 00:53:28.770
are going to be key, and a lot in terms of
the -- in terms of the lessons, the lessons

00:53:28.770 --> 00:53:31.690
of performance.

00:53:31.690 --> 00:53:36.130
There are many systems that underpin good
governance.

00:53:36.130 --> 00:53:38.990
Good governance is not simply anecdotal.

00:53:38.990 --> 00:53:44.490
There are important systems and structures
that are going to be necessary.

00:53:44.490 --> 00:53:49.980
There's a lot of human capacitation issues
that are involved.

00:53:49.980 --> 00:54:00.070
There is a need to underpin good governance
in analysis, in clear definition of what the

00:54:00.070 --> 00:54:02.490
intended outcomes are.

00:54:02.490 --> 00:54:09.420
And there's a lot that is missing yet in that
conversation, because even though citizens

00:54:09.420 --> 00:54:16.440
are finding their voice because of the global
reality that we all face today, that citizens

00:54:16.440 --> 00:54:21.410
have more access to information, you have
to ask, what kind of information?

00:54:21.410 --> 00:54:28.080
The quality of the information, the analytics
behind the information, means that there is

00:54:28.080 --> 00:54:29.460
need to engage.

00:54:29.460 --> 00:54:35.490
There are certain groups that have been trying
to do things, say, in the health sector, from

00:54:35.490 --> 00:54:36.660
the outside.

00:54:36.660 --> 00:54:40.510
Well, don't try doing that thing as an outsider.

00:54:40.510 --> 00:54:47.290
I mean, perhaps what you really need to be
doing is finding the voices from within (lose

00:54:47.290 --> 00:54:55.880
?) interest, (rely ?) in that sector on providing
them with the kind of critical knowledge that

00:54:55.880 --> 00:55:00.610
they require, because their voice has more
legitimacy than your voice.

00:55:00.610 --> 00:55:06.860
The legitimacy and the credibility of the
voice of the owners of the development problem

00:55:06.860 --> 00:55:14.050
is really an antidote to a lot of the lack
of traction that we have seen on the conversation

00:55:14.050 --> 00:55:15.950
on the demand for good governance.

00:55:15.950 --> 00:55:25.370
And I -- it's how we now take this and really
turn it into a product of help, of support,

00:55:25.370 --> 00:55:29.720
of partnership that really matters as we go
forward.

00:55:29.720 --> 00:55:36.870
You talked about ownership and budget support
and working a way -- we actually at the bank,

00:55:36.870 --> 00:55:42.940
in the Africa region, I dare say, our budget
support is an important part of our policy

00:55:42.940 --> 00:55:51.150
memo in the work with countries, because this
dramatically helps us to get countries to

00:55:51.150 --> 00:55:58.290
take charge of the important reforms that
they want to see happen in critical sectors

00:55:58.290 --> 00:55:59.440
of the economy.

00:55:59.440 --> 00:56:07.200
But you have to be careful that you don't
do budget support in a situation where there

00:56:07.200 --> 00:56:12.610
is an entire -- a lack of appetite for change.

00:56:12.610 --> 00:56:20.610
A budget support simply becomes a source of
improving resources that are available for

00:56:20.610 --> 00:56:24.140
the wrong kinds of priorities -- you don't
want to do that.

00:56:24.140 --> 00:56:32.840
I mean, you cannot be so -- (inaudible) -- about
budget support that you ignore what the outcome

00:56:32.840 --> 00:56:39.230
you are trying to achieve really would be,
and whether they're being achieved.

00:56:39.230 --> 00:56:47.510
We do a lot of rigorous -- we pay rigorous
attention to what change happens as a result

00:56:47.510 --> 00:56:49.050
of budget support.

00:56:49.050 --> 00:56:57.080
We avoid budget support fatigue where people
begin to think that they are entitled to budget

00:56:57.080 --> 00:57:05.680
support as a way of revenue mobilization,
when indeed it should be the incentive for

00:57:05.680 --> 00:57:13.550
ensuring that sustainable reforms are undertaken
so that you can exit from budget support as

00:57:13.550 --> 00:57:16.110
a country at some point.

00:57:16.110 --> 00:57:22.090
You talked about the slippage in some parts
of the -- in some countries on the continent.

00:57:22.090 --> 00:57:30.120
Certainly, a lot of them during this next
cycle of the crisis that we've seen, entered

00:57:30.120 --> 00:57:36.990
with less buffer, whether policy buffer or
fiscal buffer.

00:57:36.990 --> 00:57:42.180
And so it has meant that it's been more challenging.

00:57:42.180 --> 00:57:47.020
But just watch the policy actions of the governments.

00:57:47.020 --> 00:57:54.720
Unlike in the past, they went toward the path
of least resistance, which would be to ruin

00:57:54.720 --> 00:57:55.740
it even further.

00:57:55.740 --> 00:58:00.950
But what you see on the part of policymakers
and the leaders is, trying to manage it and

00:58:00.950 --> 00:58:09.070
finding support that would enable them, you
know, get back to the place of stability.

00:58:09.070 --> 00:58:16.460
So some of the public expenditure measures
that governments are taking are really being

00:58:16.460 --> 00:58:24.680
taken to getting this instability, the macro
instability that is setting back into a place

00:58:24.680 --> 00:58:26.020
of stability.

00:58:26.020 --> 00:58:34.470
Now, for us, we watch it and we look at how
we can support.

00:58:34.470 --> 00:58:44.440
So the support through policy, policy notes
that would enable them know the wrong options

00:58:44.440 --> 00:58:52.640
not to take, because there are times when
this situation arises, and the next thing

00:58:52.640 --> 00:58:57.450
people do is the action that comes to the
mind.

00:58:57.450 --> 00:59:00.600
So we're grounding action in evidence.

00:59:00.600 --> 00:59:06.580
And so evidence-based policy, orientation
is really taking foothold, building the critical

00:59:06.580 --> 00:59:15.210
capacity within both the ministries of finance,
working with our colleagues in the central

00:59:15.210 --> 00:59:20.280
banks with the fund is important for the policy
options they take.

00:59:20.280 --> 00:59:25.950
And then looking at public expenditure, looking
at the budget and seeing how the instrument

00:59:25.950 --> 00:59:32.890
of the budget can be better utilized for dealing
with some of these challenges that they are

00:59:32.890 --> 00:59:33.890
coping with.

00:59:33.890 --> 00:59:36.970
But a slippage is bound to happen.

00:59:36.970 --> 00:59:38.710
But I look at trend.

00:59:38.710 --> 00:59:42.450
I don't look at -- you know, I look at trend.

00:59:42.450 --> 00:59:46.550
When a slippage happens, what's the trend
in terms of policymaking.

00:59:46.550 --> 00:59:52.560
It's mostly positive in most of the countries
that are affected.

00:59:52.560 --> 01:00:00.920
For women entrepreneurs, as you know, the
bank has the gender action plan, and gender

01:00:00.920 --> 01:00:08.630
is a key part of our work in the Africa region
considering that some 50 percent of the people

01:00:08.630 --> 01:00:10.460
on the continent are women.

01:00:10.460 --> 01:00:14.831
And our particular area of focus is women
economic empowerment.

01:00:14.831 --> 01:00:18.050
And so you've come out of Benin.

01:00:18.050 --> 01:00:22.670
You know the typical Benin woman is an entrepreneur
by nature.

01:00:22.670 --> 01:00:25.360
Congenitally, they are entrepreneurs.

01:00:25.360 --> 01:00:31.160
And so we're focused on what it is for human
capital development.

01:00:31.160 --> 01:00:36.460
We're focused on education; girl child education
is key.

01:00:36.460 --> 01:00:43.320
You need a basic level of education in order
to unleash your innovative mind.

01:00:43.320 --> 01:00:44.750
That's a piece of it.

01:00:44.750 --> 01:00:51.170
The other piece of it are the doing-business
environment for women, looking at the kinds

01:00:51.170 --> 01:00:56.730
of obstacles that women entrepreneurs must
face, looking at issues involved in business

01:00:56.730 --> 01:01:02.960
registration, in access to capital, in basic
recordkeeping, in access to markets.

01:01:02.960 --> 01:01:09.840
So we are focused on that, even at the very
country, up to the project level.

01:01:09.840 --> 01:01:11.050
So it's a huge agenda for us.

01:01:11.050 --> 01:01:12.050
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: Hi, Claire Casey from Garten
Rothkopf.

01:01:12.050 --> 01:01:13.050
You spoke about both energy and a need for
energy infrastructure development, and climate

01:01:13.050 --> 01:01:14.050
change as a major risk.

01:01:14.050 --> 01:01:15.050
And I'm just wondering if you could offer
your perspective on how to reconcile those

01:01:15.050 --> 01:01:25.610
two or what the appropriate balance is in
low-carbon development given the urgency of

01:01:25.610 --> 01:01:34.730
the challenge, the scale of the challenge
and also the higher costs and risks of -- (inaudible).

01:01:34.730 --> 01:01:35.730
&gt;&gt;EZEKWESILI: OK.

01:01:35.730 --> 01:01:36.730
&gt;&gt;FRAZER: Michael.

01:01:36.730 --> 01:01:39.770
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: I'm Michael Samuels from Samuels
International Associates.

01:01:39.770 --> 01:01:43.220
Thank you very much for sharing your insights
and visions.

01:01:43.220 --> 01:01:49.470
But would you also share with us your two
biggest frustrations from your last period

01:01:49.470 --> 01:01:50.970
at the World Bank?

01:01:50.970 --> 01:01:51.970
(Laughter.)

01:01:51.970 --> 01:01:52.970
&gt;&gt;FRAZER: Jonathan.

01:01:52.970 --> 01:01:53.970
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: Hi.

01:01:53.970 --> 01:01:54.970
Jonathan Cahn, I'm with SNR Denton.

01:01:54.970 --> 01:01:55.970
I'm afraid Mr. Samuels has preempted my question.

01:01:55.970 --> 01:01:56.970
But I would obviously like to hear your response.

01:01:56.970 --> 01:02:10.090
I'll have to leave a bit earlier than I wanted
to, but was asked by Ambassador Bob Gelbard

01:02:10.090 --> 01:02:15.400
to give you his regard.

01:02:15.400 --> 01:02:18.590
&gt;&gt;EZEKWESILI: Thank you.

01:02:18.590 --> 01:02:25.310
&gt;&gt;FRAZER: And then we have eight more minutes,
so we'll finish this round and then we'll

01:02:25.310 --> 01:02:26.460
take the last three.

01:02:26.460 --> 01:02:28.030
&gt;&gt;EZEKWESILI: OK.

01:02:28.030 --> 01:02:32.940
So the climate change and development, I don't
think there has to be a trade-off.

01:02:32.940 --> 01:02:42.270
I really think that it is how much we look
at development solutions from an integrated

01:02:42.270 --> 01:02:43.660
perspective.

01:02:43.660 --> 01:02:49.950
Because as I just said, agriculture is important
for the continent, and there you see that

01:02:49.950 --> 01:02:55.250
climate change, with the (floats ?), with
the decertification, with the coastal erosion,

01:02:55.250 --> 01:02:57.190
will affect climate change.

01:02:57.190 --> 01:03:04.690
So climate-smart agriculture means that you're
going to be thinking of agriculture through

01:03:04.690 --> 01:03:14.100
the lens of the risks that are inherent in
Africa not having a good program for managing

01:03:14.100 --> 01:03:16.980
the climate change issue.

01:03:16.980 --> 01:03:23.920
When you look at Africa's opportunities for
hydropower, it's huge.

01:03:23.920 --> 01:03:28.860
Only 10 percent of it is being utilized at
the moment.

01:03:28.860 --> 01:03:35.000
Now, what are the drawbacks to unleashing
that opportunity?

01:03:35.000 --> 01:03:44.740
That has to be part of the global conversation
as far as Africa's role in helping manage

01:03:44.740 --> 01:03:47.700
the global climate change agenda is concerned.

01:03:47.700 --> 01:03:54.970
So that way becomes a win-win and not a conversation
that -- (inaudible) -- of Africa's development

01:03:54.970 --> 01:03:58.100
for better climate environment.

01:03:58.100 --> 01:04:02.490
Otherwise, if you enter that conversation
that way, then you would have people who would

01:04:02.490 --> 01:04:07.210
simply say, well, Africa did not contribute
to the global warming.

01:04:07.210 --> 01:04:12.260
It's such insignificant contribution; and
therefore, it ought not to just be called

01:04:12.260 --> 01:04:17.220
into any kind of arrangements that would -- (inaudible)
-- stem the tide.

01:04:17.220 --> 01:04:26.190
But that would be not an intelligent response
to a problem that it is already having to

01:04:26.190 --> 01:04:28.100
adapt to.

01:04:28.100 --> 01:04:29.940
So I hope that answers your question.

01:04:29.940 --> 01:04:31.190
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: I think so, yeah.

01:04:31.190 --> 01:04:32.190
(Laughter.)

01:04:32.190 --> 01:04:33.190
&gt;&gt;EZEKWESILI: Go ahead, ask a follow up.

01:04:33.190 --> 01:04:34.190
You seem to --

01:04:34.190 --> 01:04:38.070
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: Well, I guess one of the most-controversial
projects in the World Bank in recent years

01:04:38.070 --> 01:04:40.000
has been a coal plant that arguably was necessary
for feeding industry that's growing.

01:04:40.000 --> 01:04:43.950
Those choices are being made every day.

01:04:43.950 --> 01:04:50.540
And I was just wondering your perspective
on the path forward.

01:04:50.540 --> 01:04:59.050
&gt;&gt;EZEKWESILI: Well, you would see that even
in understanding the role that fossil plays

01:04:59.050 --> 01:05:06.520
in energy development and in energy, and therefore
for development, there is a clear understanding

01:05:06.520 --> 01:05:14.320
that when you're dealing with low-income,
poor countries that are only just needing

01:05:14.320 --> 01:05:21.490
energy in order to start the process of growth,
that you've got to look at it slightly differently

01:05:21.490 --> 01:05:28.140
from how you look at it when economies that
have had history of growth for so many decades

01:05:28.140 --> 01:05:32.370
continue to behave in an irresponsible manner.

01:05:32.370 --> 01:05:35.720
They are two different conversations.

01:05:35.720 --> 01:05:47.040
And so reconciling those views in a way that
you recognize Africa's own disadvantage and

01:05:47.040 --> 01:05:52.630
yet Africa's own opportunities.

01:05:52.630 --> 01:05:59.260
If you took some of the countries where you
have coal in Africa, you would find that there

01:05:59.260 --> 01:06:06.870
are countries that have mostly that as the
source of energy.

01:06:06.870 --> 01:06:10.660
That is the most they have in terms of natural
resources.

01:06:10.660 --> 01:06:19.960
So you take a country like Botswana, a country
like Botswana, it is really -- and yet it

01:06:19.960 --> 01:06:26.220
is a country that says we recognize the risks
of climate change; therefore, we will invest

01:06:26.220 --> 01:06:30.860
ourselves in looking at renewable options.

01:06:30.860 --> 01:06:34.080
That requires significant investment.

01:06:34.080 --> 01:06:39.620
As all of that conversation is going on, is
being able to balancing the conversation on

01:06:39.620 --> 01:06:46.840
climate change and development as an integrated
solution to the growth of these economies

01:06:46.840 --> 01:06:53.940
and the tackling of poverty that would enable
the conversation of a (divisive ?) and a conversation

01:06:53.940 --> 01:07:03.020
that requires immediate trade-offs of the
two agenda of development and climate change.

01:07:03.020 --> 01:07:04.020
Right?

01:07:04.020 --> 01:07:05.020
OK.

01:07:05.020 --> 01:07:08.500
And then there was a question as to my two
biggest frustration.

01:07:08.500 --> 01:07:11.910
What if it is only one?

01:07:11.910 --> 01:07:12.940
(Laughter.)

01:07:12.940 --> 01:07:22.980
I think that the -- actually, now that you
ask me, I was thinking as what -- I think,

01:07:22.980 --> 01:07:26.460
why don't I start with my greatest joy?

01:07:26.460 --> 01:07:36.240
My source of my greatest joy is to see what
is going on on the continent by virtue of

01:07:36.240 --> 01:07:39.970
the participation of the citizens in the development
process.

01:07:39.970 --> 01:07:47.200
There is no continent that could ever have
developed without the full engagement of the

01:07:47.200 --> 01:07:49.330
citizens in the process.

01:07:49.330 --> 01:07:55.340
For many decades, it was difficult to get
that level of involvement that I see today

01:07:55.340 --> 01:07:56.340
on the continent.

01:07:56.340 --> 01:08:03.870
And I have been to most of our client countries,
and my interaction with the citizens always

01:08:03.870 --> 01:08:10.440
has been the high point of my, you know, talking
with the presidents and the cabinet is not

01:08:10.440 --> 01:08:17.469
even half as exciting as when I am sitting
in a room like this and there are market women,

01:08:17.469 --> 01:08:22.120
you know, that are in that meeting, there
are civil society young activists, there is

01:08:22.120 --> 01:08:29.920
a private sector entrepreneur trying to make
an inroad into the market space and facing

01:08:29.920 --> 01:08:32.420
the obstacles and the constraints.

01:08:32.420 --> 01:08:42.739
And in that conversation, the energy that
you see, it is really a source of optimism.

01:08:42.739 --> 01:08:52.750
It can do -- it's basically a spirit that
says take the obstacles to our growth away

01:08:52.750 --> 01:08:54.949
and you would see us grow.

01:08:54.949 --> 01:09:02.239
Now, that is so really wonderful, because
it then helps me shape my conversation with

01:09:02.239 --> 01:09:08.199
the policymakers, because in discussing with
the policymakers and reporting that I met

01:09:08.199 --> 01:09:14.699
with citizens who know the solutions to their
problems, they are just asking you to get

01:09:14.699 --> 01:09:18.040
out of their way, get off their path.

01:09:18.040 --> 01:09:24.370
Take the obstacles that you put on their path
away from them and they can find solutions

01:09:24.370 --> 01:09:26.029
to their problems.

01:09:26.029 --> 01:09:29.299
Many of the citizens are actually not asking
for handouts.

01:09:29.299 --> 01:09:31.809
It is so amazing.

01:09:31.809 --> 01:09:34.500
They don't need your handout.

01:09:34.500 --> 01:09:42.100
They just need the environment to unleash
their creativity, their ideas and their solutions.

01:09:42.100 --> 01:09:51.119
That has been a very, very, very, very good
thing that I have benefited in this job.

01:09:51.119 --> 01:09:59.449
Now, frustration would have to be -- what
if I don't have any frustration?

01:09:59.449 --> 01:10:00.449
(Laughter.)

01:10:00.449 --> 01:10:06.590
I am really trying hard to think, because
I am an incurable optimist, right?

01:10:06.590 --> 01:10:10.909
So, you know, the -- I am an inpatient optimist.

01:10:10.909 --> 01:10:13.440
Ah, maybe that is my frustration!

01:10:13.440 --> 01:10:17.490
Yes, it is not happening as fast as I would
like to see it happen.

01:10:17.490 --> 01:10:28.260
I mean, I believe that since we know that
Africa must attain 7 percent economic growth

01:10:28.260 --> 01:10:36.050
rate per annum on a sustained basis in order
to make a serious dent on the poverty levels.

01:10:36.050 --> 01:10:42.269
Right now in the last decade, poverty has
declined by 1 percentage point every year.

01:10:42.269 --> 01:10:46.630
I want to see it decline by 5 percentage point
every year.

01:10:46.630 --> 01:10:52.320
But what that would require it must see investment.

01:10:52.320 --> 01:10:59.150
The investment, public investment and private
investment, that you would need in order to

01:10:59.150 --> 01:11:07.239
attain that is so huge, that when I look at
the development space, I don't see it yet.

01:11:07.239 --> 01:11:08.989
That has to be a major frustration.

01:11:08.989 --> 01:11:17.730
A major frustration is knowing that Africa
needs to raise an additional $48 billion,

01:11:17.730 --> 01:11:24.659
less the about 11 billion (dollars) that we
believe can be saved and the 17 billion (dollars)

01:11:24.659 --> 01:11:33.010
that we believe can come through better efficiencies
and policies and investment for infrastructure

01:11:33.010 --> 01:11:34.610
development.

01:11:34.610 --> 01:11:43.449
And looking at the space, looking at the flows,
it is not happening fast enough.

01:11:43.449 --> 01:11:53.230
Maybe some of you here can have the solution
to it, but that huge sum that you need in

01:11:53.230 --> 01:11:59.781
order to add many (bouts ?) of growth to the
existing growth and take it to 7 percent is

01:11:59.781 --> 01:12:02.300
a source of immense frustration for me.

01:12:02.300 --> 01:12:09.469
How, where do we find the flows, the flows
that will take care of the infrastructure

01:12:09.469 --> 01:12:11.550
problem?

01:12:11.550 --> 01:12:23.690
The other frustration, but which I hope will
be overcome, is when people, when the leadership,

01:12:23.690 --> 01:12:34.679
those amongst African leadership that still
haven't understood that Africa's great opportunity

01:12:34.679 --> 01:12:43.820
is actually the people, not the copper, not
the oil, not the gold, those leaders that

01:12:43.820 --> 01:12:56.250
are still caught in this binge for extractives
is a major source of frustration and great

01:12:56.250 --> 01:12:58.000
concern for me.

01:12:58.000 --> 01:13:05.179
Because an elite class that has not understood
yet what the citizens are saying, which is

01:13:05.179 --> 01:13:13.400
that they want education, they want investment
in the things that make them very productive

01:13:13.400 --> 01:13:15.989
and competitive.

01:13:15.989 --> 01:13:27.719
Moving that agenda would mean that the good-governance
demand would force the leaders to focus on

01:13:27.719 --> 01:13:37.070
the things that translate the rents from the
extractive industries into investments in

01:13:37.070 --> 01:13:44.530
health, in education, and in important things
that would enable them grow a drive in human

01:13:44.530 --> 01:13:50.989
capital base, because it is the knowledge
of the citizens that would really grow Africa

01:13:50.989 --> 01:13:54.409
in this next cycle of growth that we are talking
about.

01:13:54.409 --> 01:13:58.630
&gt;&gt;FRAZER: I think we have actually heard what
Obi is going to be doing next.

01:13:58.630 --> 01:13:59.882
We have heard this over and over.

01:13:59.882 --> 01:14:00.882
I think we know.

01:14:00.882 --> 01:14:02.550
We have run over time.

01:14:02.550 --> 01:14:09.070
I am going to allow the last three to ask
your question, make your statement, but I

01:14:09.070 --> 01:14:13.840
would also, you know, maybe suggest that,
Obi, if you can't answer it really quickly

01:14:13.840 --> 01:14:14.840
--

01:14:14.840 --> 01:14:15.840
&gt;&gt;EZEKWESILI: Sure.

01:14:15.840 --> 01:14:18.519
&gt;&gt;FRAZER: -- that you take it outside the
hall so that we can stay -- we are just over

01:14:18.519 --> 01:14:20.230
now, and I don't want to go too far over.

01:14:20.230 --> 01:14:21.230
So Whitney.

01:14:21.230 --> 01:14:22.869
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: Thank you, Jendayi.

01:14:22.869 --> 01:14:26.210
Thank you, Obi, for a very fulsome conversation.

01:14:26.210 --> 01:14:31.599
My question goes to your first comment about
Africa taking ownership for its own economic

01:14:31.599 --> 01:14:36.570
policy in vision, and nowhere has it been
more evident recently than in Nigeria and

01:14:36.570 --> 01:14:38.980
removal of the oil subsidy.

01:14:38.980 --> 01:14:45.280
And we saw very bold economic move, but a
very equally strong public backlash from civil

01:14:45.280 --> 01:14:46.280
society.

01:14:46.280 --> 01:14:50.409
And I am just interested in your comments
on what the government got wrong and what

01:14:50.409 --> 01:14:54.270
the government may have, you know, done right
in that context.

01:14:54.270 --> 01:14:58.139
&gt;&gt;EZEKWESILI: It is simple: pace and sequencing.

01:14:58.139 --> 01:15:00.489
Pace and sequencing, yes.

01:15:00.489 --> 01:15:01.840
&gt;&gt;FRAZER: And concise.

01:15:01.840 --> 01:15:02.840
(Chuckles.)

01:15:02.840 --> 01:15:06.710
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: Yeah, Madame Obi, many respects.

01:15:06.710 --> 01:15:08.480
First of all, I want to say many respects.

01:15:08.480 --> 01:15:11.869
You have transformed the World Bank and how
many of us relate to the World Bank.

01:15:11.869 --> 01:15:16.139
Before we used to be out there throwing eggs
at the bank and now we are inside dialoguing

01:15:16.139 --> 01:15:20.630
on important issues, so I want to congratulate
you on that.

01:15:20.630 --> 01:15:22.599
My question has to do with agriculture.

01:15:22.599 --> 01:15:25.489
And China is -- they are big time now.

01:15:25.489 --> 01:15:30.119
Middle East countries are buying up land in
Senegal and Mali, and some of us are wondering

01:15:30.119 --> 01:15:32.929
whether or not the future of Africa is being
mortgaged.

01:15:32.929 --> 01:15:37.369
Now, you came up with this document, what
is Africa's future and the World Bank support

01:15:37.369 --> 01:15:40.380
to it, which is the strategy document before
the bank.

01:15:40.380 --> 01:15:44.601
Many of us are concerned that, as you leave,
as you make your departure, the World Bank

01:15:44.601 --> 01:15:47.260
may revert back to some of its old ways.

01:15:47.260 --> 01:15:51.429
What assurances do we have that the World
Bank will continue along the path that you

01:15:51.429 --> 01:15:52.849
have outlined?

01:15:52.849 --> 01:15:54.269
&gt;&gt;FRAZER: Yes.

01:15:54.269 --> 01:16:00.829
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: I would love to hear a little
bit -- my name is David Apgar, I am actually

01:16:00.829 --> 01:16:02.070
a consultant with the World Bank.

01:16:02.070 --> 01:16:07.510
I would love to hear a little bit about the
vision for innovation and agriculture that

01:16:07.510 --> 01:16:08.510
you have.

01:16:08.510 --> 01:16:14.619
I mean, this sector is so exciting because
it is immune from two of the four classic

01:16:14.619 --> 01:16:19.970
traps that Paul Collier talked about, the
resource trap and the governance trap.

01:16:19.970 --> 01:16:25.329
And the two that you have been so involved
with personally yourself.

01:16:25.329 --> 01:16:29.030
But it is not going to -- the innovation is
not going to work the way it does in technology

01:16:29.030 --> 01:16:35.380
or in sectors that we have seen in Europe
and the U.S., it may be more exciting, but

01:16:35.380 --> 01:16:37.150
it is going to be a very different model.

01:16:37.150 --> 01:16:40.590
It is not going to be one or two little companies
that suddenly become huge.

01:16:40.590 --> 01:16:44.470
I would love to hear a little bit of your
detailed vision for that.

01:16:44.470 --> 01:16:52.110
&gt;&gt;EZEKWESILI: Well, you know, I mean, I think
that our agriculture team is quite seized

01:16:52.110 --> 01:16:58.300
on these issues of how to first start the
innovation, and that is why in recent times

01:16:58.300 --> 01:17:05.780
we have invested on a regional basis on research
and development.

01:17:05.780 --> 01:17:12.330
So one of our regional integration projects
is the productivity, agricultural productivity

01:17:12.330 --> 01:17:17.800
project: East Africa, West Africa, Central
Africa, and Southern Africa.

01:17:17.800 --> 01:17:23.449
Within that project, what we're basically
trying to do is working with the researchers

01:17:23.449 --> 01:17:29.000
and the researchers within the continent and
outside of the continent to finding these

01:17:29.000 --> 01:17:36.940
new ideas that will really make the agriculture
sector a transformational one and not just

01:17:36.940 --> 01:17:44.329
at the top level, but also in terms of organizing
the ideas of the rural farmers and the way

01:17:44.329 --> 01:17:48.760
that they have overcome some of the challenges.

01:17:48.760 --> 01:17:54.829
And so how do we enhance their productivity
through seeds, new ideas for seeds, ideas

01:17:54.829 --> 01:18:01.940
for access to -- (inaudible) -- and new sources
of technology and the integration into the

01:18:01.940 --> 01:18:03.320
market.

01:18:03.320 --> 01:18:08.989
So it is quite a huge agenda, and I hope that
that is something you can look up on our website

01:18:08.989 --> 01:18:14.230
because we do -- we're paying serious attention
to the research and development needs for

01:18:14.230 --> 01:18:17.610
that sector, for its transformation.

01:18:17.610 --> 01:18:27.170
The question on the -- on agriculture, in
terms of what is called the land acquisition

01:18:27.170 --> 01:18:35.699
by outsiders, we have worked with other partners
on something called the code for responsible

01:18:35.699 --> 01:18:37.250
agriculture investment.

01:18:37.250 --> 01:18:45.449
And this code basically lays out some principles
on the business of which government needs

01:18:45.449 --> 01:18:50.239
to be looking at its conversation on land.

01:18:50.239 --> 01:18:58.880
And we look at it as being a conversation
that must involve the voice of its citizens.

01:18:58.880 --> 01:19:09.739
The reason is that you need a good synergy
between the small, rural farmers and the large

01:19:09.739 --> 01:19:10.750
investment.

01:19:10.750 --> 01:19:13.889
Just the small, rural farmers would not cut
it.

01:19:13.889 --> 01:19:19.280
The large investments ignoring the small,
rural farmers would be a catastrophe.

01:19:19.280 --> 01:19:26.469
Therefore, you need a forum and a framework
and systems that are well designed to enable

01:19:26.469 --> 01:19:36.150
the necessary conversation between small,
rural farmers and big investors in agriculture.

01:19:36.150 --> 01:19:44.829
In a place like Zambia, we have a program
that includes -- that involves outgrower farmers

01:19:44.829 --> 01:19:51.639
(scheme ?), and the outgrower farmers are
gaining opportunities to technologies -- exactly

01:19:51.639 --> 01:19:56.880
the kind of question -- technologies and access
to imports and access to infrastructure and

01:19:56.880 --> 01:20:02.050
even access markets in ways that they were
not in the past.

01:20:02.050 --> 01:20:08.390
So how do we take advantage of the opportunities
while reducing, understanding the risks and

01:20:08.390 --> 01:20:11.340
mitigating those risks because there are risks?

01:20:11.340 --> 01:20:16.199
And then there are risks that pertain directly
to the farmer.

01:20:16.199 --> 01:20:22.070
But then there are also governance risks that
are associated with how land deals are made.

01:20:22.070 --> 01:20:29.739
So the transparency principles within the
code of responsible investment would help

01:20:29.739 --> 01:20:35.909
countries to determine how to ensure that
it is a participatory process and not one

01:20:35.909 --> 01:20:43.800
that lends itself to elite rent capture that
we have seen in the extractive industries.

01:20:43.800 --> 01:20:52.739
And then I think finally on Africa's strategy,
the strategy is so well -- is so -- is owned

01:20:52.739 --> 01:20:54.510
by many.

01:20:54.510 --> 01:20:56.750
The strategy wasn't a World Bank strategy.

01:20:56.750 --> 01:21:05.530
It is a strategy that all the spectrum of
partners on the continent and outside of the

01:21:05.530 --> 01:21:07.570
continent were part of.

01:21:07.570 --> 01:21:12.140
We simply coordinated the emergence of that
strategy.

01:21:12.140 --> 01:21:20.679
And my sense is that all of the partners that
supported the strategy, particularly our client

01:21:20.679 --> 01:21:26.179
citizens, are going to be the owners of this
strategy going forward, and that they are

01:21:26.179 --> 01:21:31.239
going to be demanding for the results that
has been promised in that strategy.

01:21:31.239 --> 01:21:39.520
And our implementation plan that accompanies
the strategy is already targeted toward ensuring

01:21:39.520 --> 01:21:42.620
that, but thanks for the compliment.

01:21:42.620 --> 01:21:45.920
Thank you very much.

01:21:45.920 --> 01:22:03.290
&gt;&gt;FRAZER: Thank you.

